## Metal-Helix Frameworks from Short Hybrid Peptide Foldamers
Metal coordinated molecular assemblies of organic templates have been extensively explored to design functionally versatile metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with a wide range of potential applications in the energy technologies, gas storage, gas separation, sensing, catalysis, biomedical imaging, organelle specific drug delivery and also as detoxification agents.[1,2] Inspired by the role of metals in the structure and functions of various types of proteins and peptides, extensive efforts have been made in the literature to design metal coordinated assemblies of proteins[3] and synthetic peptides.[4,5] The metal triggered assembly of peptides[5d,5e] and proteins[3e] offer a categorical approach to construct well defined nanostructures. However, utilization of peptides to construct ordered metal-peptide-frameworks are scarcely examined. Nevertheless, Fujita and coworkers have demonstrated the metal driven stunning protein like channels,[6] β-barrels[7] as well as peptide catenanes[8] from short α-peptides. The protein like quaternary structures have also been achieved from the metal coordinated assembly of synthetic coiled-coils[9] and collagen peptides.[10] Although metal mediated supramolecular assembly of structured peptides provided an attractive opportunity to design a variety of assemblies and nano-architectures, however the structured peptides have scarcely utilized to design porous metal-peptide frameworks. The major obstacle here is the requirement of longer α-peptide sequences to obtain stable secondary structures.
In their pioneering work, the groups of Seebach,[11] Gellman[12] and others[13] have demonstrated the definite and predictable folding properties from the oligopeptides composed of \(\upbeta-\), \(\upgamma-\), and higher homologs of α-amino acids (Foldamers). Along with the homopeptides of \(\upbeta\)-and \(\upgamma\)-amino acids, hybrid peptides of the form \((\alpha\beta)_{n},^{[14]}\) \((\alpha\gamma)_{n}\) [15] and \((\alpha\gamma)_{n}\) [16] have showed remarkable helical structures with different types of intramolecular H-bonded pseudocycles. The experimental and theoretical studies have revealed the stable 12-helical conformations from a variety of \(\alpha,\gamma\)-hybrid peptides.[15] As these \(\alpha,\gamma\)-hybrid peptides fold into stable 12-helices even in short peptide sequences,[15c,17] we hypothesized that these short helices with suitable metal coordinating ligands at the N- and C-termini can be explored to construct metal foldamer frameworks. Here we are reporting the design, crystal conformations, coordinating site dependent
