different types of silver coordinated frameworks of short \(\alpha,\gamma\) -hybrid peptide 12-helices consisting of terminal pyridyl moieties, and the utility of metal-helix-frameworks in the adsorption of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\).
The sequences of the designed short \(\alpha,\gamma\) -hybrid peptides P1 and P2 with terminal pyridyl moieties are shown in Scheme 1. Both 3-pyridyl and 4-pyridyl metal binding ligands were used to understand the impact of coordinating sites on the metal coordinated foldamer assemblies. As \(\alpha,\alpha\) -dialkyl amino acids are known to induce helical conformations in \(\alpha\) -peptide sequences,[18] we incorporated Aib residue at the N-terminus in both hybrid peptides to obtain stable helical structures. The hybrid peptides were synthesized in solution phase using standard protocols as reported earlier[17] (See ESI) and purified through column chromatography. To understand their unambiguous conformations, peptides were subjected to crystallization in various solvent combinations. Both P1 and P2 gave X-ray diffraction-quality single crystals from the solution of peptides in aqueous methanol and their structures are shown in Figure 1a and 1b, respectively. The structural analysis revealed that both P1 and P2 adopted right-handed 12-helix structures in single crystals. The helical structures are stabilized by two successive intramolecular 12-membered hydrogen bonds between the residues \(i\) and \(i{+}3\). Similarly, the helical structures stabilized by the two consecutive H-bonds have been observed in the \(\alpha\) - tripeptides of sterically constrained \(\alpha\) -amino acids.[18c] The two amide NH protons at the N-terminus and two carbonyl groups at the C-terminus of P1 and P2 are involved in the head-to-tail intermolecular H-bonding with other helices in the crystal packing. The backbone conformation of \(\upgamma\) -residues can be recognized through the torsion angles \(\phi\), \(\theta_{1}\), \(\theta_{2}\) and \(\upgamma\) as shown in Scheme 1.
As P1 and P2 were designed to coordinate with metal ions, we subjected them for crystallization with various metal salts and solvents combinations. The solution of peptides in methanol was slowly diffused into the aqueous solution of metal salts. Peptide P1 gave single crystals at the interface of peptide solution in methanol and aqueous solution containing \(\mathsf{A g B F_{4}}\). The X-ray diffraction structure of silver ion coordinated P1 is shown in Figure 2. The analysis of the crystal structure reveals a remarkable 2:2 macrocycle of peptide and silver ions. The two peptides are held together by two silver ions in a head-to-tail fashion. Upon metal coordination, P1 lost the H-bond periodicity and adopted a new conformation. In contrast to parent P1 structure, the \(N\!\cdot\) -terminal 3- pyridyl amide CO is not involved in the intramolecular H-bonding (See Fig. 1d). Only Aib1 CO is involved in the 12-membered H
