bond with the C-terminal 3-pyridyl NH. The structural analysis revealed that the \(N\!\cdot\) -terminus Aib residue adopted a left-handed helical conformation upon silver ion coordination. Similar type of reversal of the helical handedness is often observed at the C-terminal Aib residues in the right-handed \(\mathfrak{a}\) -peptide helices.[19] The conformation of L-Leu and \(\upgamma\) -Leu remain same as in the parent peptide P1 12-helix. The torsion angles and H-bond parameters of silver ion coordinated P1 are given in the Supporting Information. The two N-terminal NH groups (Aib1 NH and \(\upgamma\) -Leu2 NH) and C-terminal CO groups ( \(\upgamma\) -Leu2 CO and Leu3 CO) are involved in the intermolecular H-bonds with other P1 peptides in the crystal packing.
Single crystals of P2 obtained at the interface of aqueous solution of \(\mathsf{A g B F_{4}}\) and solution of peptide P2 in methanol gave a structure as shown in Figure 3a. The structure analysis reveals that the P2 retained its 12-helix structure. This is in sharp contrast with the results observed in the case of P1. Even though the sequences of the peptides are same except the position of the nitrogen in the pyridyl moieties, a drastic change in the peptide structure is observed upon silver ion coordination. The structure of the metal coordinated P2 is stabilized by two independent intramolecular 12-membered H-bonds. The backbone torsion angles and the H-bond parameters were found to be very similar to that of parent P2 (See ESI). No influence of silver ion coordination on the structure of the peptide P2 is observed.
Similar to parent P2, the two \(N\!\cdot\) -terminal amide NH groups and the two C-terminal amide CO groups of the silver ion
coordinated P2 12-helix are involved in the head-to-tail intermolecular H-bonding. The infinite intermolecular head-to-tail H-bonding as well as head-to-tail silver ion coordination of P2 are shown in the Figure 3b. Overall, the silver ion coordination drives the infinite polymerization along the horizontal direction, while the head-to-tail intermolecular H-bonds drives the helical polymerization along the vertical direction. Thus, stable P2 12-helix provided a unique opportunity to build supramolecular architectures using the combination of metal coordination and H-bonds.
The silver coordination and H-bonds driven porous supramolecular assembly of P2 12-helix is shown in Figure 3c. The pore size of the self-assembled structure was found to be \(5.9\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}.\) The silver ion coordination of the 12-helix has offered a crystalline, porous network similar to many metal-organic frameworks.[1] Further, the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) reveals that no observed mass change of P2Ag framework up to \(200\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (See ESI) indicating its stability against the temperature. The porous nature of the metal-helix framework motivated us to examine its ability to capture the greenhouse gas carbon dioxide.[20] In order to understand the gas adsorption property of metal-helix framework, peptide P2 was synthesized in gram scale and crystalized in the presence \(\mathsf{A g B F_{4}}\) . The powder of metal-helix framework obtained after grinding the single crystals was subjected to \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) capture at \(195~\mathsf{K}\) . The PXRD studies confirm that there is no change in the metal-helix framework in the powder (Figure 4). The graph depicting the capture of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) by the silver-helix framework is shown in the Figure 4. Indeed as anticipated the silver ion-helix framework showed the adsorption of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) . Overall, these results suggested that the \(\mathfrak{a},\gamma\) -hybrid peptide 12-helices with 4- pyridyl derivatives can be used as templates to design porous metal-foldamer frameworks.
In conclusion, we have demonstrated the silver ion mediated supramolecular assembly of bis(pyridyl) terminated \(\mathfrak{a},\gamma\) -hybrid peptide 12-helices. The 12-helical conformation in the single crystals is stabilized by two independent 12- membered intramolecular H-bonds. Upon silver ion coordination, the tripeptide 12-helix with terminal 3-pyridyl groups undergoes a structural distortion to give 2:2 macrocycle. In contrast, peptide P2 with terminal 4-pyridyl groups retained its original 12-helix conformation upon silver ion coordination. Through silver ion coordination and intermolecular head-to-tail H-bonding, the 12-helix of P2 displayed a porous metal-helix framework. Although the sequence of the peptides are same, a subtle change in the metal coordinating site showed a drastic change in the supramolecular assembly. The stable 12-helices can be further explored to construct metal-helix frameworks with other metal ions. In addition to the metal ions, the length of 12-helices can also be varied to design various types of porous functional metal-helix frameworks. The remarkable metal mediated networks of short \(\mathfrak{a},\gamma\) -hybrid peptide 12-helices reported here open wide opportunities to explore them to construct new family of metal-helix frameworks.
